act invalid input/condition) | expected C result | [ ] |\n|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `main` / first `%d` | EOF before the first conversion (empty input or whitespace followed by EOF) | `scanf` returns EOF; `x=0`, `y=0`; no stdout; `main` returns `0` | [ ] |\n| 2 | `main` / first `%d` | First non-whitespace byte cannot begin a decimal integer | `scanf` returns `0`; `x=0`, `y=0`; no stdout; `main` returns `0` | [ ] |\n| 3 | `main` / second `%d` | First conversion succeeds, then EOF occurs before the second conversion | `scanf` returns `1`; `x` has the first value, `y=0`; output follows the `y==0` path; `main` returns `0` | [ ] |\n| 4 | `main` / second `%d` | First conversion succeeds, then the next non-whitespace byte cannot begin a decimal integer | `scanf` returns `1`; `x` has the first value, `y=0`; output follows the `y==0` path; `main` returns `0` | [ ] |\n\nGeneric FFI boundaries are not applicable: the sole public entry point is\n`int main(void)` and accepts no pointers, lengths, or enum values. Zero integer\nvalues are valid and are covered in `CONFIGS.md`. Decimal values outside C\n`int` range are excluded because C leaves conversion overflow undefined.\n# Configuration Surface\n\nBuild-time axes:\n\n- Cargo declares no features and CMake declares no options.\n- The only valid build combination is the empty feature set:\n  `--no-default-features --features \"\"`.\n\nRuntime/API axes mechanically present in `c_src/src/main.c`:\n\n- Public entry point: `main` only (`foo` is `static`).\n- Input parser: two decimal `int` conversions through `scanf(\"%d %d\", ...)`.\n- Loop/label predicates: `x > 0 || y > 0`, `x == 1 && y == 4`, `x > 0`,\n  `y == 0`, and `x < 3`.\n- There are no runtime options, modes, flags, element types, byte-order\n  choices, pointer arguments, length arguments, or enum arguments.\n\nRows below are the pruned cross-product of conversion shape and loop predicate\nstates that the C code treats differently. Randomized values stay in small\nranges because output volume is proportional to the values.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---|----------------|--------------------------------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `main` | Two valid integers with `x <= 0` and `y <= 0`; outer loop is initially false and stdout is empty | [ ] |\n| 2 | `main` | Two valid integers with `x > 0` and `y == 0`; each outer iteration takes `x > 0` then `y == 0` | [ ] |\n| 3 | `main` | Two valid integers with `x == 0` and `y > 0`; `x > 0` is false and `x < 3` repeatedly jumps to `label1` | [ ] |\n| 4 | `main` | Two valid integers with `x < 0` and `y > 0`; same label cycle as row 3 with a negative `x` state | [ ] |\n| 5 | `main` | Exact pair `x == 1`, `y == 4`; initial shortcut jumps directly to `label2` and skips one `x` action | [ ] |\n| 6 | `main` | Both positive, initial `1 <= x <= 3`, excluding `(1,4)`; after decrement, `x < 3` cycles through `label1` in one outer iteration | [ ] |\n| 7 | `main` | `x >= 4`, `y > 0`, with `y` exhausted while post-decrement `x >= 3`; execution transitions to the `y == 0` outer-loop path | [ ] |\n| 8 | `main` | `x >= 4`, `y > 0`, with enough `y` to reach post-decrement `x < 3`; execution transitions into the inner `label1` cycle | [ ] |\n| 9 | `main` | `x > 0`, `y < 0`; `y == 0` never becomes true and the C operation does not terminate; compare a bounded output prefix and nontermination | [ ] |\n| 10 | `main` | Valid conversions split across spaces/tabs/newlines, with optional signs and trailing non-converted bytes; `%d %d` consumes the same two values | [ ] |\n\nInvalid conversion shapes are listed separately in `ERRORS.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
